Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <regex>
#include <string>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html = markdown;
    std::regex pattern(R"(#\s*(\w+)\s*(.*)");
    std::smatch match;

    while (std::regex_search(html, match, pattern)) {
        std::string tag = match[1];
        std::string content = match[2];
        html = html.replace(match.length(), "<h" + tag + ">" + content + "</h" + tag + ">");
    }

    return html;
}
```

This function uses a regular expression to match the markdown headings and replace them with HTML tags. The regular expression pattern `#\s*(\w+)\s*(.*)` matches a line that starts with a `#` followed by zero or more whitespace characters, followed by a word (for the heading level), followed by zero or more whitespace characters, followed by the content of the heading. The function then replaces each match with `<h tag>content</h tag>`, where `tag` is the heading level captured by the regular expression.

